    "question": {
      "difficulty": "EASY",
      "content": "Write a Python function that builds a **term frequency dictionary** from a small corpus of raw text strings. This is a common first step in text preprocessing pipelines, where noisy input must be cleaned before any downstream analysis.\n\nThe preprocessing steps applied to each document are:\n\n```\n1. Lowercase all characters\n2. Remove all characters that are not a letter (a-z) or a space\n3. Split on whitespace to produce tokens\n4. Remove tokens that appear in the provided stopwords list\n5. Count how many times each remaining token appears across all documents\n```\n\nThe result is a single dictionary mapping each surviving token to its total count over the entire corpus.\n\n**Parameters**\n\n- `corpus` (list[str]): A list of raw text strings (documents) to process\n- `stopwords` (list[str]): A list of words to exclude after tokenization\n\n**Requirements**\n\n- Apply all five steps in the order listed above\n- Do not use any external NLP libraries (no NLTK, no spaCy). Use only standard Python string methods and the `re` module\n- Tokens that become empty strings after cleaning must be discarded\n- Return tokens sorted alphabetically as keys in the output dictionary (use a regular Python dict with keys in sorted order)\n\n**Returns**\n\n- dict[str, int]: A dictionary mapping each cleaned, non-stopword token to its total count across the corpus, with keys in alphabetical order",
